  "content": "If you see an \"Over Temperature\" warning on the display and hear a buzzer, your Yamaha 210FSH engine is overheating. Immediately reduce engine speed, return to shore or maneuver to a safe location, and check for water discharge at the cooling water pilot outlets on the starboard side of the hull. While the engines are running (especially when applying throttle), confirm that water is coming out of those outlets. If no water is seen, cooling water may not be circulating. <PIC> If the overheating engine cannot be fixed, shut off that engine and use the other properly running engine to return to shore at no-wake speed (8 km/h, 5 mph) to avoid water flooding the non-operating engine. If both engines overheat, operate both just above idle while returning to shore, or get a tow if possible.",
  "images": ["Manual09_95", "Manual09_96"]
}

small_answer.raw_response:
{
  "content": "1. Moor the boat securely so it will not drift away. 2. With the engine stopped, look in all directions, then start the engine. 3. Run the engine at idling speed for 6 minutes or more (add 5 minutes if the ambient temperature is 20°C (68°F) or less). 4. Stop the engine. 5. Open the engine hood. 6. Loosen the oil tank filler cap, remove it, and wipe the attached dipstick clean. 7. Screw the oil tank filler cap into the filler hole until it stops, then remove it again and check that the engine oil level is between the minimum and maximum level marks on the dipstick <PIC>. 8. If the level is significantly above the maximum mark, consult a Boat dealer. If below the minimum mark, slowly add engine oil. 9. Repeat steps 6–8 until the oil is at the proper level. 10. Securely install the oil tank filler cap and turn it until it stops. 11. Repeat the procedure for the other engine. 12. Close the engine hood.",
  "images": ["Manual09_196"]
}

